// The exported C symbols of ONNX Runtime, one novo-lang declaration
// each.  There are two of them.
//
// ONNX Runtime exposes its C API through one symbol, `OrtGetApiBase`.
// It answers the address of a structure of two function pointers.  The
// first of those, `GetApi`, answers the address of the `OrtApi`
// structure, which holds every other call of the C API as a field of
// function-pointer type.  Creating an environment, creating a session,
// building a tensor, running a model and releasing an object are all
// fields.  None of them is a name the loader can resolve, so none of
// them can be an `@ffi` declaration.
//
// The second symbol, `OrtSessionOptionsAppendExecutionProvider_CPU`,
// is a plain C function in the CPU provider factory, and it is
// declared here because it is a symbol.  It takes an
// `OrtSessionOptions`, which only a field of the table creates, so
// nothing in this package can produce an argument for it.
//
// A pointer is an `Int` and zero is null.  `ptr.read_word` reads a
// function pointer out of a structure.  The novo-lang foreign function
// interface cannot call a function through its address.  The README
// says what a program that wants to run a model has to do instead.
//
// The examples in these comments are fenced `novo ignore`, so `novo
// doc` does not compile them.  A compiled example links against
// libonnxruntime, and the link fails where that library is not
// installed.

// Get the address of the `OrtApiBase` structure.
//
// This is the entry point of the C API.  The answer is the address of
// a structure the library owns for as long as it is loaded, and there
// is nothing to release.
//
// The structure is two eight-byte function pointers, in this order.
//
// | Offset | Field | What it is |
// | --- | --- | --- |
// | 0 | `GetApi` | takes a version number and answers the address of the `OrtApi` table, or 0 when the runtime does not support that version |
// | 8 | `GetVersionString` | takes nothing and answers the address of a string such as `"1.17.3"` |
//
// Both are addresses of C functions, and neither can be called from
// novo-lang.  Reading them with `ptr.read_word` shows that the library
// loaded.
//
// ```novo ignore
// let base = libonnxruntime.ort_get_api_base()
// let get_api = ptr.read_word(base)
// ```
pub @ffi(library = "onnxruntime", symbol = "OrtGetApiBase")
fn ort_get_api_base() -> Int [ffi]

// Add the CPU execution provider to a set of session options.
//
// `options` is an `OrtSessionOptions`.  `use_arena` is non-zero to let
// the provider use an arena allocator and 0 to allocate each time.
// Answers 0 on success.  On failure it answers the address of an
// `OrtStatus`, which the caller releases with `ReleaseStatus`, a field
// of the table.
//
// The function is declared in `cpu_provider_factory.h`.  It cannot be
// used on its own, because an `OrtSessionOptions` is created by
// `CreateSessionOptions`, which is a field of the `OrtApi` table.
//
// ```novo ignore
// let status = libonnxruntime.ort_session_options_append_execution_provider_cpu(options, 1)
// ```
pub @ffi(library = "onnxruntime", symbol = "OrtSessionOptionsAppendExecutionProvider_CPU")
fn ort_session_options_append_execution_provider_cpu(options: Int, use_arena: Int) -> Int [io, ffi]
